{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"20f13c50defc0c86","name":"card_unit_35th_infantry_regiment","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Poland/units/card_unit_35th_infantry_regiment","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"35th INFANTRY REGIMENT","text":"When an enemy unit moves into the frontline, your units get +1+1.","flavorText":"At the outbreak of WWII  35th Infantry Regiment was part of the 9th Infantry Division, also known as the 9th Infantry \"Wielkopolska\" Division, and was one of the major divisions of the Polish Army during the war. The division was formed in 1919 and played a significant role in the defense of Poland during the German invasion in September 1939, including the Battle of Bzura, where it was defeated by the German forces. The 9th Infantry Division defended a 70-kilometer line in Polish Pomerania as part of the Pomorze Army. They faced attacks from three Wehrmacht divisions and withdrew to the Cekcyn area. The Germans broke their defense line, and the division retreated to the outskirts of Bydgoszcz after crossing the Brda River. The 35th Infantry Regiment defended against the German 2nd Motorized Infantry Division, while the 22nd Infantry Regiment faced the 32nd Infantry Division along the Brda River. Following their defeat, the 9th Infantry Division regrouped in the woodlands to the north of Bydgoszcz where they came under attack from the Luftwaffe. With no means to defend themselves and lacking air support, the division disintegrated and ceased to exist. The sole exception was the 35th Infantry Regiment, which managed to successfully break out of the German encirclement on September 5th. Later in the war, the 9th Infantry Division was reconstituted as part of the Polish Armed Forces in the West and participated in the Italian Campaign, contributing to the liberation of Italy.","faction":"Poland","type":"infantry","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":3,"operationCost":1,"attack":1,"defense":5},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_35th_infantry_regiment","title":{"Namespace":"card_poland","Key":"card_unit_35th_infantry_regiment_title","SourceString":"35th INFANTRY REGIMENT","LocalizedString":"35th INFANTRY REGIMENT"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::infantry","attack":1,"defense":5,"range":1,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Poland","kredits":3,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_poland","Key":"card_unit_35th_infantry_regiment_text","SourceString":"When an enemy unit moves into the frontline, your units get +1+1.","LocalizedString":"When an enemy unit moves into the frontline, your units get +1+1."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_poland","Key":"card_unit_35th_infantry_regiment_history","SourceString":"At the outbreak of WWII  35th Infantry Regiment was part of the 9th Infantry Division, also known as the 9th Infantry \"Wielkopolska\" Division, and was one of the major divisions of the Polish Army during the war. The division was formed in 1919 and played a significant role in the defense of Poland during the German invasion in September 1939, including the Battle of Bzura, where it was defeated by the German forces. The 9th Infantry Division defended a 70-kilometer line in Polish Pomerania as part of the Pomorze Army. They faced attacks from three Wehrmacht divisions and withdrew to the Cekcyn area. The Germans broke their defense line, and the division retreated to the outskirts of Bydgoszcz after crossing the Brda River. The 35th Infantry Regiment defended against the German 2nd Motorized Infantry Division, while the 22nd Infantry Regiment faced the 32nd Infantry Division along the Brda River. Following their defeat, the 9th Infantry Division regrouped in the woodlands to the north of Bydgoszcz where they came under attack from the Luftwaffe. With no means to defend themselves and lacking air support, the division disintegrated and ceased to exist. The sole exception was the 35th Infantry Regiment, which managed to successfully break out of the German encirclement on September 5th. Later in the war, the 9th Infantry Division was reconstituted as part of the Polish Armed Forces in the West and participated in the Italian Campaign, contributing to the liberation of Italy.","LocalizedString":"At the outbreak of WWII  35th Infantry Regiment was part of the 9th Infantry Division, also known as the 9th Infantry \"Wielkopolska\" Division, and was one of the major divisions of the Polish Army during the war. The division was formed in 1919 and played a significant role in the defense of Poland during the German invasion in September 1939, including the Battle of Bzura, where it was defeated by the German forces. The 9th Infantry Division defended a 70-kilometer line in Polish Pomerania as part of the Pomorze Army. They faced attacks from three Wehrmacht divisions and withdrew to the Cekcyn area. The Germans broke their defense line, and the division retreated to the outskirts of Bydgoszcz after crossing the Brda River. The 35th Infantry Regiment defended against the German 2nd Motorized Infantry Division, while the 22nd Infantry Regiment faced the 32nd Infantry Division along the Brda River. Following their defeat, the 9th Infantry Division regrouped in the woodlands to the north of Bydgoszcz where they came under attack from the Luftwaffe. With no means to defend themselves and lacking air support, the division disintegrated and ceased to exist. The sole exception was the 35th Infantry Regiment, which managed to successfully break out of the German encirclement on September 5th. Later in the war, the 9th Infantry Division was reconstituted as part of the Polish Armed Forces in the West and participated in the Italian Campaign, contributing to the liberation of Italy."},"operationCost":1,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::BrothersInArms","isInPermanentPool":true,"EffectType":"ECardEffectTypes::Trigger","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardLocationMoved"],"draftBaseWeight":0.9,"hasGuard":true,"soundBlowUp":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'Explosion_